@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+I lead enterprise systems the way I'd want them led if I were the CFO or the head of HR waiting on a platform to actually work: with a clear view of what the business needs, a bias toward automating the boring parts, and enough discipline around governance that nobody gets a surprise at audit time. At SK Life Science I spent close to eight years building and running the application ecosystem behind a $1B commercial operation - SAP, Ariba, Veeva, Salesforce, Snowflake - and the thing I'm proudest of isn't any single platform, it's that Finance, HR, Legal, and Supply Chain all trusted the data coming out of it. That trust gets built by treating business leaders as the actual owners of their processes and treating IT's job as removing friction, not adding process for its own sake. I run a hands-on shop. I still expect to be in the room for solution design, not just reviewing status decks, and I hold my teams and vendors to the same standard - clear ownership, measurable outcomes, no drama at go-live. On automation specifically, I've pushed AI into places where it earns its keep rather than where it's fashionable: security triage, expense anomaly detection, next-best-action analytics for a field team, all with humans reviewing outputs and validated systems remaining the system of record. That's the balance NeoGenomics is describing for HR, Finance, and Supply Chain - automation that reduces overhead without weakening control. What I'd bring to this role is a track record of doing exactly that inside a regulated company, plus the instinct to know when a platform decision is actually a governance decision in disguise, and to get the right people in the room before it becomes a problem.